To read all data from process.stdin in Node.js, you can use the following code snippet:

```javascript
let inputData = '';

process.stdin.on('data', (chunk) => {
  inputData += chunk;
});

process.stdin.on('end', () => {
  // All data has been received, inputData contains the complete input
  console.log('Received input:', inputData);
  // You can now process inputData as needed
});
```

In this code:

- We declare a variable inputData outside the event listeners to store the accumulated input data.
- The data event listener is attached to process.stdin. Whenever there is input data available, the data event is emitted, and the associated callback function is executed. The chunk parameter contains a portion of the input data. We append this chunk to the inputData variable.
- The end event listener is attached to process.stdin. When there is no more data to read from the input, the end event is emitted, indicating that we have received all the input data. At this point, the inputData variable contains the complete input.
- Inside the end event listener, you can process the inputData as needed. For example, you can parse it, perform calculations, or take further actions based on the received input.

This approach allows you to read and accumulate all the data from process.stdin until the end of the input is reached, making it suitable for processing large amounts of input data or handling user input in interactive scripts.
